HolRefute provides counterexample generation for HOL4, with theorem
certification enabled by default.  It has a Quickcheck (QC) backend for
executable goals, native narrowing, and a Kodkod-based model-finder (MF)
backend.  Exhaustive and random QC testing run over three interchangeable
execution substrates:

* Compute interprets test plans with computeLib.  It is the compatibility
  substrate and supports registered custom and abstract generators.
* Cv compiles a fuel-bounded HOL test loop and executes it with
  Thm.compute.  Goals outside its first-order fragment are inapplicable.
* NativeSML extracts the executable fragment to SML and compiles the test
  loop in process.  It is the preferred fast substrate on Poly/ML.

`:rat` has a built-in QC generator (`Refute_EvalRat`), registered
unconditionally at Refute's load time.  Its candidates are a custom
generator (`Refute_Gen.GenCustom`), so -- exactly like any other
user-registered custom generator -- Cv and NativeSML report it
inapplicable ("custom generator registered") and `Auto` falls through to
Compute, the only substrate that can host it.  The same load also installs
a computeLib fragment that decides `+ - * / ~ inv < <= > >= =` on closed
`:rat` literals via proof-time conversions rather than compset rewriting
(`ratLib.RAT_CALC_CONV`/`fracLib.FRAC_CALC_CONV` plus arithmetic
side-condition discharge); an open `:rat` term is left stuck rather than
unfolded into `rep_rat`/`rep_frac` form, and rat numerals are otherwise
unaffected.  Because that fragment is added to the global compset, `EVAL`
decides closed `:rat` literals afterwards too, not only Refute's own
substrates.  A decided comparison yields `T` or `F`, but a decided
arithmetic result is a raw `abs_rat (abs_frac (n, d))` value, not
reduced to lowest terms; the `n // d` display is the QC generator's
candidate shape, not an output normal form.  As with any finite QC
sample over an infinite type, `:rat` QC never reports
`NoCounterexample` for a goal quantified over `:rat` variables:
exhaustive and random search both report `Unknown` instead, however
large the size or count.  A closed `:rat` goal is not such a sample --
reducing it to `T` is a proof -- and can decide to `NoCounterexample`.

`:real` has a built-in QC generator too (`Refute_EvalReal`), registered
the same way and with the same `GenCustom`/Compute-only consequences.
Unlike `:rat`, real's arithmetic and comparisons
(`+ - * / < <= > >= = abs pow`) were already decided on closed literals
by realLib's own global-compset installation (`realSimps.sml`), loaded
transitively -- except `n / &0` for a nonzero `n`, which realSimps'
`elim_common_factor` conv leaves stuck rather than reducing.  The only
gap that fragment otherwise left was `inv`, which `Refute_EvalReal`
adds, declining (rather than reducing) on a zero argument to match
`/`'s existing refusal on a zero divisor.  A decided value is
`real_of_num n`/`real_neg (real_of_num n)` for an integer, or
`real_div` of such a numerator over a positive `real_of_num`
denominator -- the same shape realSimps' own literal rules already
produce, so a QC candidate is displayed exactly as a user would write
it.  A finite rational sample can never exhaust the uncountably many
reals, so `:real` QC never reports `NoCounterexample` for a goal
quantified over `:real` variables either; a closed `:real` goal can
still decide to `NoCounterexample`, for the same reason as `:rat`
above.

QC's own genuine, certified counterexamples reach into negative and
non-integral territory routinely: e.g. the ordered-field
monotonicity trap `a <= b /\ c <= d ==> a * c <= b * d`, false at both
`:rat` and `:real` once `a` and `c` may be negative, is refuted with a
readable negative witness at both types.  Division by a zero literal
and `rat_minv`/`inv` at zero are sound but incomplete: `:rat`'s and
`:real`'s compset fragments both decline on these rather than
mis-deciding them (matching `/`'s own zero-divisor refusal), so a
*closed* goal built from them reports `Unknown`, not
`NoCounterexample`.  The two types are not symmetric here, though.
`inv (0 : real) = 0` is a genuine theorem (`REAL_INV_0`), so `:real`'s
decline is an incompleteness -- a fact the compset fragment fails to
use.  `:rat`'s `rat_minv` has no such fact to miss: `frac_minv`
constructs `abs_frac (0, 0)` at zero, outside `frac`'s defining
predicate `0 < SND f`, so `rat_minv 0` is genuinely unspecified and
declining is the only correct behaviour, not a gap.  The model finder,
unlike either compset fragment, does evaluate zero-divisor division
inside its Frac encoding: it reports a `Genuine` `1 / 0 = 0` at both
types.  At `:real` that settles the *false* closed goal `1 / 0 = 1`,
because `1 / 0 = 0` is itself a real theorem (`REAL_INV_0` again, via
`real_div`).  At `:rat` the same encoding move instead *picks* the
value `0` for a division HOL leaves completely unspecified, on a goal
that has no fact either way -- sound under the same trust model that
lets Refute rely on Kodkodi's arithmetic exactly as Nitpick relies on
Kodkod, and never stricter, but a choice rather than a deduction.

`:'a |-> 'b` (finite maps) is a third built-in case, but registered
through a different mechanism than `:rat`/`:real`'s single `GenCustom`
instance: `Refute_Gen.register_generator_family {tyop, constructors,
canonical}` (exposed as `Refute.register_generator_family`) declares a
type operator's constructors (`FEMPTY`, `FUPDATE`) once, and the
generator fires on demand at whatever concrete instance a goal needs,
built as an ordinary `GenDatatype` over those constructors rather than a
bespoke spec.  Because it is a `GenDatatype`, not a `GenCustom`, Cv and
NativeSML are not refused outright by `has_registered_generator`; each
independently declines it by name -- Cv's `check_in_fragment` checks
`Refute_Gen.family_for` before attempting any cv translation, and
NativeSML's `ensure_type` rejects it as soon as it finds fmap's
`TypeBase` entry has no constructors -- falling through to Compute under
`Auto` exactly as an ordinary inapplicable substrate would.  The
registration always sets `exhaustive = false`: a finite map's key type
is typically infinite, so no finite sample of update chains ever
exhausts the space, and QC must never report `NoCounterexample` for a
goal universally quantified over an `fmap`-typed variable -- only a
*closed* fmap fact (no such variable) can decide that way, the same
asymmetry `:rat`/`:real` already have.  `Refute_Gen.enumerate` never
fires for fmap either: a family spec's `exhaustive = false` makes
`datatype_cardinality` short-circuit to `NONE` before `FUPDATE`'s
recursion is examined, and `enumerate` gates on that cardinality; the
`Genuine` results an exhaustive-only search still finds come from a
separate, always-incomplete generator private to the Compute substrate
that never calls `enumerate`.  The Narrowing strategy is unreachable for
fmap for the same underlying reason as NativeSML's decline above:
extraction rejects every fmap-typed prefix variable by name before any
narrowing shape is built, so fmap's `exhaustive` flag, unlike an
ordinary recursive datatype's, is never narrowing-load-bearing.
`FEMPTY`/`FUPDATE` are also registered with
`Refute_Core.nonexecutable_constants`'s executability check
(`Refute_Gen.is_family_constructor`), trusted the same way an ordinary
datatype's `TypeBase.is_constructor` already is; without this, a goal
mentioning `FUPDATE`/`FEMPTY` literally in its text -- as opposed to
only through a generated variable -- would be rejected as "not
executable" regardless of the registered generator, since `FUPDATE` is
`[nocompute]`-tagged in `finite_mapScript.sml` and fmap's `TypeBase`
entry has no constructors of its own.  `canonical`, when supplied, is a
display-only `term_postprocessor` applied at
`Refute_QC.record_candidate_with`'s single choke point for every
reported binding; the rewrite it installs never reaches testing or
certification, both of which always see the untouched `env` this
mechanism closes over, independent of when the rewrite runs relative to
them.  fmap's is `canonical_fmap_chain`, which dedups an update chain
like `FEMPTY |+ (1,2) |+ (1,3)` to the semantically identical
`FEMPTY |+ (1,3)` *in place* -- a later `aconv` key shadows an earlier
one, at the earlier one's position -- with no sort: sorting would need
to compare keys as values, which `Term.compare` cannot decide, so two
chains that denote the same map via non-`aconv` keys are not guaranteed
to display identically.  `Refute_Gen.lookup_family_canonical ty` exposes
the registered rewrite for a given instance type; `NONE` means no family
registered a canonical form for that type (or none is registered at
all).  Like the model finder's own `register_term_postprocessor`, a
callback that raises a non-interrupt exception or changes the term's
type is ignored and the raw candidate is kept; interrupts re-raise.

An unconfigured call has one 10-second search budget covering preprocessing,
backend admission, compilation, testing, model translation, and solver calls.
All concurrent backends share its absolute deadline.  Mandatory cleanup still
runs after the budget expires, so the observed return time can be slightly
longer.  The default QC size and model-finder card rows use iterative
deepening: 10 is their initial window, not a terminal bound.  `upd_size` and
`upd_card` deliberately select fixed bounds; use `upd_iterative_size` and
`upd_iterative_card` to select a different initial adaptive window.

`NoCounterexample` is reserved for a search that covers the whole semantic
space: no counterexample is possible at any larger size.  A clean fixed-bound
search that does not cover every relevant type returns `Unknown`, with a
reason such as `no counterexample within the tested scopes` and the searched
frontier.  Exhaustive QC and narrowing can establish totality for finite
generator spaces, and the model finder can do so for a constant preprocessed
formula or exact finite scopes -- except when a generated problem's own
encoding carries a value-position `unknown` (the `wfrec'` ersatz row, see
"Known limitations and follow-ups" below), which vetoes the totality claim
outright regardless of how many scopes exhaust.  `NoCounterexample` is
decisive in a backend
race and stops the other searches, just like a counterexample at the declared
certainty ceiling.

Kodkod with `upd_falsify false` reports the distinct `Model` and `NoModel`
outcomes.  Backend selection never changes the meaning of `Counterexample`
or `NoCounterexample`.  In a mixed run model search is auxiliary, so a
decisive refutation result can preempt it.

Set a substrate with `Refute.upd_substrate`.  The default, `Auto`, walks all
registered substrates in priority order; the built-in order is NativeSML,
Cv, then Compute.  Auto falls through when a substrate is inapplicable.  An
explicitly selected inapplicable substrate reports `Unknown`; it never
silently changes substrate.  Selection applies to both exhaustive and random
QC backends.  A custom or replacement substrate can open a smart-generator
gate only by compiling the smart plan itself, and Auto executes that exact
selected compilation.  At Refute trace level 2 or higher, Auto reports
selection and fallthrough reasons.  Every counterexample also records the
selected substrate in its `substrate` field.

Random testing uses one specified 64-bit generator and consumption order on
all substrates.  `Refute.upd_seed (SOME n)` therefore reproduces the same
candidate stream independently of substrate.  Runs without an explicit
seed use a deterministic session stream which advances between runs.

Smart generators are enabled by default.  A `Hol_reln` premise, or a
Boolean function with exhaustive Horn-shaped equations, is mode-compiled to
a positive CPS enumerator for exhaustive testing on all three substrates.
`upd_depth` sets its fixed per-plan fuel (default 10); it does not add a
schedule loop.  Compute and cv define the same structurally recursive HOL
list enumerators (cv then translates those definitions); native SML preserves
the same clause and premise order.  Because fuel bounds every smart
enumeration, Enum- or SmartGuard-bearing terminal exhaustion is always
reported as incomplete.  Ignored counterexamples are matched by structural
environment equality, not by stream position.  Setting
`upd_smart_generators false` restores generate-and-guard planning, and
`upd_reorder_premises` controls mode-analysis reordering.

Native narrowing is active by default as the `narrowing` backend.  It uses
`size` as its initial refinement-depth window and supports prenex existential
goals.  In fixed mode that window is the complete requested search; in
iterative mode narrowing compiles later depths one at a time and releases each
compiled window before advancing.
`upd_finite_functions true` (the default) represents function inputs by
finite `ffun` update chains, with `cfun` as the constant-only fallback for
higher-order domains.  Counterexamples reconstruct `ffun` values as HOL
`UPDATE` chains.  Setting the option to false restores the direct function-
type inapplicability diagnostic.

Execution substrates are untrusted accelerators.  When `certify=true` (the
default), Refute_Cert re-evaluates an exhaustive or random QC candidate's
instantiated proposition with computeLib.  A re-evaluation that proves the
proposition true discards the candidate; one that proves it false attaches a
theorem certifying its negation.  If direct re-evaluation is stuck, Refute may
also normalize the closed proposition to PNF and replay an all-universal
prefix using the candidate's exact generated bindings.  Mixed or existential
PNF prefixes are not handled by this ordinary-QC fallback.  An inconclusive
attempt leaves the substrate-derived verdict unchanged with `cert = NONE`.
Native narrowing uses Refute_Cert_Narrow to replay its recorded case tree;
ground and partial candidates fall back to Refute_Cert evaluation.  With
`certify=false`, Refute
skips QC certification, so an otherwise genuine hit has `cert = NONE` and is
reported as uncertified.  Certification is monotone: enabling it cannot
weaken the certainty obtained with it disabled, and can only discard a false
positive or add a theorem.  Candidates already known to be stuck or
incomplete during testing are not promoted by either mode.  `certainty`
records the semantic strength of the result, while `cert` records whether
Refute constructed a HOL theorem.  The two are independent axes: a result
may be `Genuine` with `cert = NONE`.

Model-finder certification replays reconstructed values in the HOL kernel.
It first prefers values with matching Skolem provenance, then ordinary model
bindings and reconstructed type values.  If those do not close the negated
goal, replay may use bounded constructor synthesis, exhaustive one-layer
datatype cases, regular single-property datatype induction, propositional
tautology proving, Presburger arithmetic, and linear real arithmetic
(`realLib.REAL_ARITH`, gated on the goal containing a `real`-typed
subterm).  Lists, ordinary records and enums, and regular recursive
datatypes such as `zoo_tree` are supported by these paths.  Every exported
certificate has the exact negated closed goal, no hypotheses, and no
untrusted oracle tag.

Partially reconstructed model-finder values have a separate private replay
view.  Its `?`, `_`, and `...` fragments are fresh schematic variables with
per-reconstruction provenance; the public raw and formatted models retain the
traditional marker notation and never expose the private names.  Schematic
values are witness candidates, not assumptions or claims that a finite scope
exhausts a HOL type.  Replay can therefore certify a function update whose
unknown fallback is not inspected, while the displayed fallback remains `?`.

If schematic replay is stuck, a deterministic bounded portfolio may replace
the remaining holes by typed closed candidates.  All attempts share one fuel
budget and absolute deadline.  A false completion can yield an audited
certificate, but a true chosen completion only causes replay to try the next
candidate: it never discards the Kodkod model.  Whole-formula truth discards a
model only in the exact initial attempt, when the instantiated formula is
hole-free.  Exhausted, capped, or failed completion search preserves both the
model and its encoding-derived certainty.

Replay is deterministic and fail-closed.  Fuel, candidate, constructor,
case-branch, induction, leaf-normalization, and deadline limits can make an
otherwise real model remain uncertified.  Mutual and nested recursion,
codatatype induction or coinduction, and abstract typedef or quotient
transport remain unsupported unless a dedicated trusted theorem-backed path
is present.  An unsuccessful replay never weakens the model finder's
encoding-derived certainty.  The `qc.certify` setting controls Quickcheck
candidate certification; it does not disable this optional model-finder
replay.

SETTLED DECISION — do not revisit without the owner.  Refute trusts Kodkodi
exactly as Isabelle/HOL's Nitpick trusts Kodkod, and must never be stricter.
A model-finder verdict is decided by the *encoding*, not by whether a HOL
theorem was built: `Refute_ModelFinder_Model.fallback_certainty` maps the
translation's soundness and exactness flags onto `Genuine`, `QuasiGenuine`
and `Potential`, mirroring Nitpick's genuine / quasi-genuine / potentially
spurious.  A reconstructed model may therefore be `Genuine` with
`cert = NONE`, exactly as a QC hit is under `certify=false`.  Requiring a
certificate for a genuine model-finder result collapses the two axes, makes
the model finder inconsistent with the QC path, and starves the
`max_potential` budget so that found models are discarded and reported as
`Unknown`.  A failed optional certification attempt likewise leaves the
encoding-derived verdict unchanged.  The certificate-required rule was
introduced on 2026-07-29 (3948a7bed), its
consequences re-baselined into the tests (2db19131b), and both were reverted;
tests expecting `Genuine` with `cert = NONE` from kodkod are correct as
written.  The same rule reappeared as a budget (92c24de7e) and was removed
too: `max_potential` bounds models that may be spurious because their
*encoding* is unsound, so only the liberal problems spend it.  Every model
of a sound problem is a real result — `Genuine` when the encoding is exact
and `QuasiGenuine` when it is inexact — and is charged to `max_genuine`
regardless of whether kernel certification succeeds.
Refute uses no oracle tags.  Cv performs all per-call definitions and
translations inside a full theory snapshot/revert bracket.  Success,
failure, timeout, and interruption paths leave no Refute-created type,
constant, theorem, or binding in the user's theory; if safe cleanup is
unavailable, Cv reports that the goal is inapplicable instead.

Backend races stop when a backend proves `NoCounterexample`, or when a
counterexample reaches the highest certainty that any selected backend
declares reachable for the current configuration and instances.
`Refute.register_backend` gives existing backends the safe, conservative
ceiling `Genuine`.  A backend with a tighter bound can use
`Refute.register_backend_with_ceiling`.  Its callback must return an upper
bound: overestimating only forgoes an early stop, whereas underestimating can
hide a stronger result and violates the backend contract.

Backend admission and execution use a worker pool local to each Refute call.
The default `upd_sequential false` therefore starts one worker per selected
backend even in an ordinary session whose process-global thread count is one.
Refute does not change that global count, so unrelated HOL tools keep their
usual scheduling policy.  `upd_sequential true` is the complete serial
opt-out.  Top-level backend workers do not reduce a backend's own internal
parallelism; in particular, the model finder's `max_threads = 0` retains its
automatic Kodkod/Kodkodi setting, so the default can oversubscribe a machine.

The unused-assumption facade consists of `check_unused_assms`,
`find_unused_assms`, and `print_unused_assms`.  Each takes a `config option`
first.  `NONE` derives a QC-only configuration from the current configuration;
`SOME cfg` supplies the base configuration and preserves its backend selection.
Every individual probe forces a sequential, quiet, abort-potential,
expectation-free profile.

User interface
--------------

`REFUTE_TAC` is the normal proof-development interface.  It runs every
selected backend, prints the result, and returns the original goal unchanged:

    Proof
      REFUTE_TAC >>
      (* prove the unchanged goal *)
    QED

Three specialized tactics restrict only the search selection inherited from
`the_config`:

* `QUICKCHECK_TAC` selects exhaustive, random, and narrowing search;
* `NARROWING_TAC` selects only native symbolic narrowing; and
* `MODEL_REFUTE_TAC` selects only the relational model finder.

Most proof scripts should use `REFUTE_TAC`.  The specialized names are useful
when a particular counterexample shape or backend capability is wanted.

Advanced callers can change options for one tactic application without
mutating `the_config`:

    REFUTE_TAC_WITH
      [upd_search (Only [Narrowing]),
       upd_size 7,
       upd_timeout 5.0]

The elements are ordinary `config -> config` updaters and are applied from
left to right, so the last update of a field wins.  `REFUTE_TAC_WITH` reads
`the_config` when the tactic is applied.  `REFUTE_CONFIG_TAC cfg` instead
uses exactly `cfg`, which is preferable for reproducible tools and tests.
Neither form silently changes `quiet` or `expect`.

`upd_size n` and `upd_card rows` pin reproducible hard bounds.  Their
iterative counterparts store an initial window and restore adaptive search.
An iterative card row must be a consecutive prefix `[1, ..., n]`; sparse and
per-type candidate lists remain available in fixed mode.  The mode itself is
the `size_mode` field of `qc_config` and the `card_mode` field of
`mf_config`; `#size` and `#card` hold the bound or the initial window.

Only the primary size coordinates deepen.  Smart-generator `depth`, random
`iterations`, `finite_type_size`, word `widths`, constructor `max`, binary
`bits`, fixpoint `iter`, `bisim_depth`, result quotas, and `tac_timeout`
remain independent fixed controls.

A type variable in the index position of a word type is a *width* variable
and is instantiated to an fcp numeral type (`:1`, `:2`, ...) rather than to an
`rf` carrier, which no substrate can size.  `upd_widths` sets the row tried,
default `[1, 2, 3, 4]`; entries must lie between 1 and 32.  Carrier and width
roles are paired by clamped instance index, so instance `k` uses carrier
`rf (min (k, finite_type_size))` and the `k`-th configured width, and enough
instances are generated to try every configured width at least once.  This is
unrelated to the model finder's `upd_bits`, which sizes *binary integers*.

By default this one carrier/width pair covers *every* type variable of a
goal at once: a goal with two type variables is tested with both at the
same type, and every report names the one cardinality that instance
tested.  `upd_instantiate` pins named type variables to fixed types
instead, leaving every variable no entry reaches on the shared
carrier/width exactly as an empty list would.  A `NONE` key is the
fallback for every *non-width* variable no `SOME` entry names, so a
`NONE`-only assignment pins all of them and collapses the search to one
instance -- unless the goal has a symbolic word width, since a width
variable takes a pin only from its own `SOME` entry (`NONE` never
reaches it, because carriers and width numerals are disjoint value
spaces) and otherwise keeps varying over the configured widths.  A width
variable must therefore be pinned to a concrete numeral type, not an
ordinary carrier.  Naming a type variable the goal lacks, a width
variable a non-numeral type, or a pin value that still carries type
variables of its own, is a configuration error reported as `Unknown`.
Because pinning only narrows which monomorphic proxies are tested, not
what the polymorphic goal itself means, a completely covered but
still-polymorphic goal degrades to `Unknown` exactly as it does without
pins; `upd_instantiate` can turn `Unknown` into a `Genuine`
counterexample but never into `NoCounterexample`.  `upd_instantiate`
scopes to QC and narrowing; the model finder is unaffected and has its
own per-type-variable updater, `upd_card [(SOME ty, [n])]`.

A free variable at a typedef type `t` (`new_type_definition`, abstraction
`abs`, representation type `rty`, characteristic predicate `P`) usually
leaves QC with nothing to test: `t` has no generator and none of the
built-in fallbacks apply.  `upd_use_subtype true` rewrites `!x : t. B x`
to `!r : rty. P r ==> B (abs r)`, contracting every `rep (abs r)` redex
the substitution creates back to `r`, and reports a hit's binding for
`x` as `abs`-of-the-tested `r`, never `r` itself -- one rewrite of the
goal serves every QC strategy and substrate, narrowing included: it
compiles a PNF formula rather than plans, so it takes the rewritten
goal open and closes the fresh representation variables into its own
prefix itself.
`TYPE_DEFINITION P rep` makes `abs` restricted to `{r | P r}` a bijection
onto `t`, so the guard is the soundness condition, not an optimisation:
dropping it lets a representation value outside `t`'s image manufacture
a spurious refutation.  Whether a filtered representation search counts
as exhausted follows the same completeness bookkeeping as any other QC
search; transport adds no bespoke exhaustion rule in either direction.
In practice that makes transport a counterexample-search aid rather
than a verification route: the generator ranges over the whole
representation type and does not know the characteristic predicate
bounds it, so a true transported goal reports `Unknown` unless its body
collapses to a ground truth under normalization.
A genuine transported hit never certifies: replaying the original
closure needs the untransported `x`, but a transported search's
environment only ever binds the representation-typed `r`, so the
report always carries `Genuine` certainty with no attached certificate
-- proving it by kernel replay would require evaluating the very
`rep (abs r)` redex the contraction exists to avoid.
Transport is a fallback of last resort: a registered generator or an
`abstract_generator` registration for `t` wins, a TypeBase datatype for
`t` wins, and only when none of those apply and `t` is itself a
harvestable typedef (`Refute_ModelFinder_HOL.harvest_typedef`) with a
real `TYPE_DEFINITION` theorem behind it
(`Refute_ModelFinder_HOL.raw_typedef_data`) does the flag take effect.
That last requirement excludes the model finder's synthetic frac and
fmap typedef entries: `harvest_typedef`'s own `is_typedef`
short-circuit admits them past its `incompatible` test, but they carry
no `TYPE_DEFINITION` theorem, so nothing licenses the guard or the
contraction -- and for frac the synthetic `abs` is not even a constant.
It also does not reach inside another type: a free
variable of type `t list` keeps today's `NoGenerator`/`Unknown`
behaviour, since transporting it would require lifting the predicate
and generator through the list structure, which this fallback does not
do.  Default `false`, matching Isabelle's `use_subtype`; the model
finder never sees the rewrite -- it already handles typedefs natively,
through the same harvested registry.
Checking whether `t` is a harvestable typedef calls `harvest_typedef`
itself, so a `use_subtype`-enabled QC-only search that reaches this
fallback permanently populates the model finder's global typedef
registry as a side effect -- the same lazy harvest the model finder
performs on its own, hence idempotent and settling no verdict
differently than the model finder's own harvest would, but a QC-only
call site now has that observable effect.

Backend selection is typed for the four built-ins:

    datatype backend_choice =
        Exhaustive
      | Random
      | Narrowing
      | ModelFinder
      | RegisteredBackend of string

    datatype search =
        AllBackends
      | QuickcheckBackends
      | Only of backend_choice list

`RegisteredBackend` is the escape hatch for an extension registered under a
string name.

The same per-call update list works outside the tactic layer through
`refute_with` and `refute_goal_with`.  `apply_updates updates cfg` constructs
an exact configuration, while `current_config updates` applies them to the
current interactive configuration.

Module layout:

* Refute_Core: shared types, configuration, registry, and orchestration.
* Refute_Gen: generator derivation and enumeration support.
* Refute_EvalRat: the built-in :rat QC generator and computeLib fragment.
* Refute_EvalReal: the built-in :real QC generator and its inv fragment.
* Refute_Eval: the substrate seam, registry, and common random generator.
* Refute_EvalCompute, Refute_EvalCv, and Refute_EvalSML: substrates.
* Refute_Extract: in-memory HOL-to-SML extraction for NativeSML.
* Refute_QC: QC test planning and random/exhaustive execution.
* Refute_QC_Narrow: native narrowing compilation and scheduled execution.
* Refute_Cert: EVAL-based counterexample certification.
* Refute_Cert_Narrow: narrowing case-tree validation and proof replay.
* Refute_Forl and Refute_ForlSat: the FORL/Kodkodi process bridge and SAT
  solver configuration.
* Refute_ModelFinder and Refute_ModelFinder_*: preprocessing, scope and
  representation selection, Kodkod translation, and model reconstruction.
* Refute_Unused: unused-assumption probes and theory sweeps.
* Refute.sig and Refute.sml: the public API facade.

The `examples/` directory holds executable theory scripts showing typical
usage, from first steps to the model finder, the SML automation API, and
which library types each half of Refute can serve; `examples/README` indexes
them.  Run `Holmake examples` here to build them with the standard HOL heap
and their declared `Ancestors` and `Libs`.  They are documentation rather
than tests: extending Refute through the registries below is described here,
not demonstrated there.

The `tests/` directory holds the selftest and nothing else: `selftest.sml`,
its golden files under `tests/goldens/`, and the fixture theories the
selftest loads (`refuteTableZoo`, `refuteUnused`, `refuteHarvestType`,
`refuteHarvestAbs`).  `Holmake` here recurses into it; keeping the fixtures
there keeps them out of the installed signature namespace.

Model-finder surface
--------------------

The model finder supports native polymorphic scopes, monotonicity and smart
finitization, specialization and boxing, binary integers, machine words,
(co)inductive predicates, codatatypes, quotients, typedefs, finite maps,
value pinning, model formatting, and incremental multi-model search.  The
principal options and their defaults are:

| updater | default | meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `upd_card` | adaptive 1--10 | carrier sizes; updater pins fixed |
| `upd_max` | `[(NONE, [~1])]` | constructor carrier bound; `~1` unbounded |
| `upd_specialize` | `true` | specialize static higher-order arguments |
| `upd_box` | `[(NONE, NONE)]` | box functions and pairs when useful |
| `upd_binary_ints` | `NONE` | select binary integers when useful |
| `upd_bits` | `[1, ..., 10]` | candidate binary integer widths |
| `upd_star_linear_preds` | `true` | use exact closure for linear predicates |
| `upd_iter` | default depth row (below) | fixpoint unrolling depths |
| `upd_bisim_depth` | `[9]` | codatatype bisimulation depth; `~1` disables it |
| `upd_finitize` | `[(NONE, NONE)]` | finitization selected by monotonicity |
| `upd_whack` | `[]` | constants whose definitions are ignored |
| `upd_need` | `NONE` | values that must occur in the model |
| `upd_tac_timeout` | `0.5` seconds | inner tactic budget; `0` skips |
| `upd_format` | fallback `[1]` | group curried function arguments |
| `upd_max_potential` | `1` | maximum potential models returned |
| `upd_max_genuine` | `1` | maximum genuine models returned |
| `upd_merge_type_vars` | `false` | fuse all MF type-variable scopes |

The default `iter` row is `[0,1,2,4,8,12,16,20,24,28]`.  A `NONE` key in a
row is its fallback.  For smart Boolean rows, a `NONE` value asks Refute to
choose; `SOME true` and `SOME false` force the choice.  A `max` row is keyed
by constructor rather than by type: it bounds how many values that
constructor may contribute to its type's carrier.
`tac_timeout` bounds the two inner proof attempts the model finder makes
per problem: the monotonicity calculus, and the well-foundedness proof for
an inductive predicate.  Either one exceeding it is not an error; the
answer degrades to "not monotonic" or "not well-founded", which costs
scopes or precision but never soundness.  A budget of `0` is decided
rather than timed, so it skips both attempts outright instead of racing
them against a deadline that has already passed.
The `max_potential` and `max_genuine` limits are global budgets.  Requesting
more than one model forces an incremental SAT solver when necessary, and a
genuine result ends the potential-model search.  With
`upd_merge_type_vars true`, the model finder type-instantiates every goal,
evaluated-term, and needed-term type variable to the alphabetically first one
before building its context.  This reduces independent scope blocks, at the
cost of potentially losing models that require the variables to have
different cardinalities.

A word type of concrete width `w` is a native numeric carrier, not a
datatype and not a typedef: it has exactly `2^w` atoms, atom `j` denoting
`n2w j`, and models print in the surface literal form.  The carrier is exact
and never searched, so a scope never sizes it; a width whose carrier would
exceed 1024 atoms refuses by name.  Encoded directly are word literals,
`n2w`, `w2n`, `+`, `-`, unary `-`, `*`, `~`, `&&`, `||`, `??`, `<<`, `>>`,
`>>>`, and the unsigned and signed orders.  Width-changing operations
(`w2w`, `sw2sw`, `word_extract`, `word_concat`) and the bit-permuting
remainder (rotations, `word_reverse`, `word_log2`, ...) are not encoded and
are refused by name, so an inconclusive report never passes for a proof.
Word arithmetic runs in Kodkod's integers, which read an atom's value from
its universe index; binary integers replace those bounds, so a problem
mentioning a word turns smart binarization off, and forcing it with
`upd_binary_ints (SOME true)` refuses the word instead.  A width left as a
type variable has no carrier at all — such a goal is refuted through the
monomorphic width instances described above.  A `cart` type that is not a
word is not encoded.

`:char` is the same kind of carrier: exactly 256 atoms, atom `j` denoting
`CHR j`, exact and never searched, and models print as character literals.
Encoded directly are character literals, `CHR`, `ORD`, and `char_lt`,
`char_le`, `char_gt`, `char_ge`; everything else on characters unfolds
through `ORD`, and `:string` stays an ordinary `:char list`.  Read instead as
the typedef `{n | n < 256}` it is defined as, a character would cost a `:num`
carrier of 256 values and put the numeral 256 in the axiom set, which alone
switches binarization on; as a carrier it costs neither.  A character is read
off the sequential integer bounds just as a word is, so it turns smart
binarization off too, and `upd_binary_ints (SOME true)` refuses the character
operation by name.  `ORD` and `CHR` are total only where the `:num` carrier
holds the code, so a goal over a large code needs `card num` above it.

A typedef over `:num` or `:int` also turns smart binarization off.  Binarized,
its bound is a numeral needing a carrier atom of its own, and the scope search
grows the carriers together: the scopes where the abstract type is large
enough are exactly the ones where the numeral is missing, so all of them are
unsatisfiable.  Forcing `upd_binary_ints (SOME true)` still binarizes, and
reaches a model only at a `card num` named above the bound.

Registrations are session-local ML state and create no theory content:

* `Refute.register_codatatype {tyop, case_const, constructors, witness}`
  validates the shape of `tyop`, `case_const` and `constructors`
  (non-empty, distinct constructors, each declaring - at its own
  declared type, not merely at whatever instance the caller applied it -
  a `tyop`-headed result type; a case constant with a unique valid
  scrutinee position, in any argument; the type applied to distinct type
  variables) and registers.  When `tyop` is a type the datatype database
  already knows as a free datatype, every registered constructor must
  also be one of that type's database constructors, by key; this rejects
  an impostor constant declared at the right type without needing a
  witness.  For a type the database does not know (hand-rolled
  codatatypes such as `zoo_stream`), this imposes nothing, and shape
  validation alone cannot tell codata from an ordinary inductive
  datatype: constructor and case types look the same either way, so
  `witness = NONE` accepts the registration on trust, exactly as
  Isabelle's own `register_codatatype` does.  The built-in registrations
  below are themselves trusted this way too, but through a different
  path - the lazy harvest that finds them calls the shape check directly
  rather than `register_codatatype`, so no witness ever applies to them.
  `witness = SOME thm` narrows what a caller can get away with further:
  after stripping `thm`'s outer `!`s, its conclusion must be `?x. body`,
  `body` an equation with one side `aconv` to `x` (either orientation)
  and the other a constructor application `C a1 ... an`, where `C`'s key
  matches a registered constructor and `x` is `aconv` to some argument
  reached by walking `C`'s constructor spine - descending into an
  argument only when its own head is again a registered constructor -
  not merely free somewhere in it.  Every registered constructor's
  *declared* result type is `tyop`-headed, so the key match makes `C`
  literally that declared constant; `C` need not be fully applied, so
  `x`'s own type need not be `tyop`-headed (e.g. an argument of function
  type).  `x`'s type also need not be the generic instance, so
  `|- ?xs : num llist. xs = llist$LCONS 0 xs` is as conclusive a witness
  as a polymorphic one.  Mutual-cycle witnesses
  (`?x y. x = C ... y ... /\ y = C ... x ...`) and HOL4's per-type
  bisimulation theorems (`llist_bisimulation` and friends) are not of
  this shape and are not accepted; a caller holding one passes
  `witness = NONE`.  Together, the witness rules out generic shape
  defeats and the database cross-check rules out impostor constants for
  types the database knows; beyond that, the constructor list remains
  the caller's assertion.  Lazy built-ins cover `llist`, `ltree`,
  `itree`, `itreeTau`, `lbtree`, and `path` when their theories are
  present.
* `Refute.register_quotient {qty, rty, abs, rep, equiv_thm}`
  registers the abstract and representation types and constants.  `equiv_thm`
  must be hypothesis-free and either a `QUOTIENT equiv abs rep` theorem
  mentioning the registered `abs` and `rep`, or a total equivalence
  `!x y. R x y <=> (R x = R y)`.  Its shape selects the encoding: a
  `QUOTIENT` theorem gets the sound partial encoding, whereas a total
  equivalence drops its then redundant domain axiom.
* `Refute.register_typedef {ty, abs, rep, absrep_thms}` registers an existing
  HOL typedef.  `absrep_thms` is either `[conjunction]`, the exported
  conjunction of the two bijection laws, or `[first, second]`, the two
  halves `!a. abs (rep a) = a` and `!r. P r = (rep (abs r) = r)` supplied
  separately in either order; either form must agree with the type's
  `<tyop>_TY_DEF` theorem.
* `Refute.register_term_postprocessor pattern f` installs a bottom-up,
  display-only model-value transformation for every type matched by
  `pattern`; `f` must preserve the term's type.  Re-registering the same
  pattern replaces rather than duplicates it.  All matching transformations
  compose: general patterns run before strictly more specific patterns, and
  otherwise older registrations run before newer ones.  Thus a specific or
  later incomparable transformation has final precedence.  One registry
  snapshot is used for an entire model, callback failures or type changes
  leave that display term unchanged, and certification always uses untouched
  raw terms.  `Refute.lookup_term_postprocessor ty` reports this composed
  transformation.  Rational and real Frac encoding are both registered by
  default after their normalization-faithfulness corpora passed.  The
  idempotent `Refute.register_frac_type_rat ()` and
  `Refute.register_frac_type_real ()` restore or refresh their respective
  encoding and built-in transformation: `rat`'s displays reconstructed
  Frac atoms with `rat_cons`'s `//` syntax, while `real`'s prints them as
  real literals -- `3 / 4`, `3` for a unit denominator or a zero
  numerator, never `rat_cons`'s `//`.  Example 12 shows the registered
  rational encoding in use on a `rat` goal; both registration calls are
  exercised by `selftest.sml`.  This is the model finder's own Frac
  display path, unrelated to the `:rat` QC generator (`Refute_EvalRat`,
  described above): that generator builds `rat_cons`-or-bare-numeral
  candidates directly, with no postprocessor involved.  Certification
  (`certify=true`, the default) is attempted on a real Frac counterexample,
  but not exactly as on any other: `frac$abs_frac` is `int # int -> frac`
  (`src/rational/fracScript.sml`), so it retypes natively to neither
  `real` nor `rat`, and replay sees an opaque reserved variable for both
  alike.  For a qualifying `:real` binding, that variable's value is
  dropped from the certification env rather than authorized, and the
  atom's genuine real-literal value (recovered the same way the display
  transformation reads it) is offered to replay separately as an
  untrusted candidate hint, never as a trusted term; a linear
  real-arithmetic leaf (`realLib.REAL_ARITH`) can then close goals
  computeLib alone cannot.  A nonlinear instance still comes back
  inconclusive (`cert = NONE`, `certainty` unchanged) -- valid and
  expected under the trust model, not a blocker.  `rat` counterexamples
  are unaffected: the candidate-hint mechanism is deliberately scoped to
  `real`-typed atoms by a structural check (type, application shape, and
  reserved-name check), so a `rat` binding's identically-shaped reserved
  atom instead fails that scope check outright -- the certification env
  is never built, `certification_copy` returns `NONE`, and `certify`
  falls back to `Keep base`, exactly as before this mechanism existed.
  Extending it to `rat` would need a rat-capable arithmetic leaf and is a
  recorded follow-up, not implemented here.  A successful real-Frac
  replay reports `model = NONE`, and any `evals` row whose evaluated
  term still has a free variable (e.g. the dropped binding's own
  variable) is dropped rather than displayed half-evaluated -- only
  kernel-established values are shown.

Structural registration types use distinct type variables as parameters.
Abs, rep, case, and constructor terms must be existing constants.  A later
compatible registration for the same type operator replaces the session
entry.

Kodkodi support is Unix-only and requires a Java runtime plus the standard
Unix `setsid` utility (used to isolate and clean up its process group).  Obtain
`kodkodi-1.5.7.tar.gz` from the Isabelle components mirror at
<https://isabelle.in.tum.de/components/> and unpack it locally.  Set
`HOL4_KODKODI` to the resulting `kodkodi-1.5.7` component directory.  Its
`jar/` directory must contain `antlr-runtime-3.1.1.jar`, `kodkod-1.5.jar`,
`kodkodi-1.5.7.jar`, and `sat4j-2.3.jar`.

HolRefute constructs the Kodkodi JVM command itself.  Java is selected from
`HOL4_JAVA_EXECUTABLE`, then `JAVA_HOME/bin/java`, then `java` on `PATH`.
As an escape hatch, `HOL4_KODKODI_EXEC` can instead name a complete
standalone launcher command.  It takes precedence and is passed to the
shell verbatim.  Configuration checks are lazy and never start a JVM while
modules are loaded or built.  The launcher shipped in the component's
`bin/` directory depends on Isabelle and is not the standalone override.

`SAT4J` is the portable default.  JNI solvers are advertised only when their
individual shared libraries are present under `jni/<platform>`: MiniSat,
CryptoMiniSat, and Lingeling require `libminisat`, `libcryptominisat`, and
`liblingeling`, respectively.  External solver settings retain Isabelle's
environment variables, including `MINISAT_HOME`, `CRYPTOMINISAT_HOME`,
`ZCHAFF_HOME`, `RSAT_HOME`, `RISS3G_HOME`, `BERKMIN_HOME`, and
`BERKMINALLOY_HOME`; `BERKMIN_EXE` selects a nondefault BerkMin executable.

The prepared development host uses
`$HOME/.local/opt/Isabelle2025-2/contrib/kodkodi-1.5.7` and
`$HOME/.local/bin/java`; other machines need a local Kodkodi 1.5.7 component
and a Java runtime.  No network access is needed at run time.

HolRefute is included in the normal tree build only on Poly/ML; non-Poly
builds skip the directory.  Its level-2 selftests include the MF acceptance,
differential, soundness, and theory-hygiene suites, plus cross-substrate QC
outcome and candidate-stream conformance and Cv export-cleanliness checks.

The refute_simp, refute_psimp, and refute_unfold theorem sets are defined by
the support theory and used by model-finder preprocessing.

`Refute.upd_iter` selects model-finder unrolling depths.  A mutual fixpoint
group shares one iterator row: members are checked in `_cases` theorem order,
and the first member with an explicit row wins before the default row is used.
Predicates introduced by `Hol_reln` and `Hol_coreln` are recognized through
the IndDef registries and support direct well-founded equations or joint
least/greatest-fixpoint unrolling.  A definition whose right-hand side is
directly headed by `fixedPoint$lfp` or `fixedPoint$gfp` but is absent from
those registries is recognized too (`fixpoint_definition_parts`,
`derived_fixpoint_group` in `Refute_ModelFinder_HOL.sml`): its unrolling
equation is derived from `fixedPointTheory.lfp_fixedpoint` or
`gfp_greatest_fixedpoint` once `monotone` on the functional is discharged
by `InductiveDefinition.MONO_TAC`, never by hand-building the unrolling
equation.  The derived equation supersedes the definition's own
presentation (`is_raw_equational_fun`), which would otherwise leave the
encoder with `p = lfp F` and suppress the polarity-correct unrolling.
A functional whose monotonicity cannot be discharged is not refused --
unfolding `lfp_def`/`gfp_def` directly stays sound, merely more
expensive -- it is only diagnosed, at `Refute` trace level 2.  Mutual
hand-rolled fixpoints are out of scope: a tupled mutual definition is
still recognized and sound as the single tupled predicate it syntactically
is, but is not unrolled as a joint group the way `Hol_coreln` output is.

Known limitations and follow-ups
--------------------------------

The model finder lazily harvests typedef and quotient metadata when an
unregistered Abs/Rep occurrence is encountered.  Loading or exporting a theory
only records its invalidated harvest entry; pending theories are indexed on
first demand.  If validated harvesting fails, it returns `Unknown` rather than
risk a spurious genuine result; an explicit registration can then supply the
metadata.  Harvesting is deliberately demand-driven rather than an eager scan
on every theory load.  Typedef harvesting accepts either the exported
conjunction of the two bijection laws or the two halves saved separately,
provided both live in the same theory; halves split across two theories are
not paired.  `Refute.harvest_registrations ()` is an opt-in
alternative: it sweeps every theory in `Theory.ancestry`, oldest first, once,
attempting the same lazy typedef and quotient harvest for every type operator
those theories declare, and returns only what that call newly registered.  A
caller who would rather pay the scan upfront, once, than have it triggered
piecemeal by whichever goals happen to mention a type first can call it; the
demand-driven lazy harvest remains the default and is unaffected.

The normalized Frac/ersatz encoding for `rat` is registered by default.
The analogous `real` encoding on the same Frac carrier is also registered
by default; it excludes `real_ge` and `real_gt` for symmetry with the
`rat` encoding, which excludes `rat_geq`
and `rat_gre`.  The exclusion is harmless because each is an ordinary,
non-recursive `Definition` naming its `<`/`<=` dual: `is_equational_fun`
keeps it un-inlined, and `equational_fun_axioms` hands the Definition to
`add_axiom`, whose right-hand side reaches the ersatz table on the next
unfolding pass.  Measured at matched scope and boundary values, this
reaches an identical verdict either way: a goal phrased purely with
`real_ge`/`real_gt` (or `rat_geq`/`rat_gre`) refutes at the same scope,
with the same witness, as its definitional `<=`/`<` dual -- no
refutation power is lost by leaving them out of the ersatz table.
Relation inverse and composition (`inv` and `O`) already have native
monotonicity rules, with dedicated verdict, scope, soundness, and
certified argument-order coverage.  Relation identity (`Id`)
is HOL4 notation for `(=)` rather than a constant of its own, so there is
nothing to unfold: it is eta-expanded to a partial application of equality
and encoded as a singleton set.  The `Iden` nut constant and its Kodkod
translation exist but are never produced; emitting `iden` directly would be
a performance refinement, not added power.  `relation$WF` is second-order
and out of reach directly, so it goes through the same builtin-ersatz path
as `CARD`: `WF` is swapped for `refuteTheory`'s `wf' R = (!x. ~TC R x x) /\
(FINITE (\p. R (FST p) (SND p)) \/ unknown)`, exact on a genuinely finite or
polymorphic domain but only `Potential` over an intrinsically infinite one
such as `num`, where a bounded scope can never decide the `FINITE`
disjunct.  `relation$WFREC` is likewise second-order, and unfolding it
straight through `the_fun` (its own Hilbert-choice fixpoint,
`relationScript.sml`) risks the same empty-extension danger as `min$@`
below; it is swapped instead for `refuteTheory`'s `wfrec'`, whose body
reads `wf_wfrec R Fn x` and is paired with a `wf_wfrec -> wf_wfrec'`
ersatz row of its own.  Unfolding hands `add_axiom` the literal
recursive equation `wf_wfrec' R Fn x = if WF R then
Fn (RESTRICT (wf_wfrec' R Fn) R x) x else unknown` rather than an
unrolling over an unconstrained function, and the knot survives the
default specialized path too, where it is re-tied only on a later pass
(`mf_wfrec_ersatz_ties_the_knot`, `selftest.sml`).  The `WF R` guard is
itself swapped for `wf'`, so it decides exactly on a finite or
polymorphic domain like any other `WF` occurrence, but its `unknown`
else-branch is a syntactic part of every `wf_wfrec'` occurrence's
harvested axiom regardless of how that check resolves, so any goal
reaching this row costs the search `NoCounterexample` outright, not
only over an infinite domain: an `unknown` at a value position is
exactly as restrictive on the liberal side of a search as on the sound
side, so an exhausted search can no longer be trusted to mean no
counterexample exists (`unknown_value`,
`Refute_ModelFinder_Kodkod.sml`).
`pred_set$SUM_IMAGE` is defined by an `ITSET` fold, whose
unfolding the encoder handles badly: measured against a 30s budget, it
sizes scopes over `num itself -> num` and `(num -> bool) # num`,
exhausts four of the six emitted scopes of `SUM_IMAGE (\e. e) {0;1;2} =
3` where the ersatz encoding exhausts all six, and times out outright on
a symbolic-set goal such as `SUM_IMAGE f {a} = f a`.  So it too takes an
ersatz row, `refuteTheory`'s `sum' f s = if FINITE s then
SUM (MAP f (@xs. set xs = s /\ ALL_DISTINCT xs)) else 0`:
the same distinct-witness-list construction `card'` counts on, and it
disagrees with `SUM_IMAGE` past `FINITE` the same way `card'` disagrees
with `CARD`.  Both rows are liberal, not faithful: the `min$@` guard
below (see "Hilbert choice") keeps a missing witness list from ever
turning that disagreement into a spurious `Genuine` result -- see
`card'_def`'s comment (`refuteScript.sml`) for the full argument.
`pred_set$SUM_SET` (`= SUM_IMAGE I`) needs no row of its own: it is an
ordinary defined constant, so the encoder constrains it by its harvested
equation, and the equation's body reaches the `SUM_IMAGE` row.

`:'a |-> 'b` (finite maps) also get a builtin-ersatz route rather than
the genuine `fmap_TY_DEF` typedef: that typedef's own representation
type is recursive and its well-foundedness proof does not run
automatically, so unrolling it directly is the same dead end `WFREC`
avoids above.  `refuteTheory`'s `is_fmap'`/`abs_fmap'` register fmap's
own typedef instead, over representation type `'a -> 'b option` -- a
plain function type the scope search already handles exactly.
`is_fmap'` carries a `FINITE`-guarded `unknown` escape like `wf'` above,
for the same syntactic reason.  Measured: dropping the `\/ unknown`
disjunct from `is_fmap'_def` leaves the level-1 selftest at 939 OK/0
failures, byte-identical to the pre-ablation baseline -- no pin,
including one that reaches `Genuine` over an infinite key type
(`finite_map_model_finder_reaches_genuine`, `selftest.sml`), is
sensitive to the escape's presence.  Its necessity is therefore not
established by anything in this suite; it is kept for the same reason
`wf'` carries one, not because a test demonstrates a need for it (see
`refuteScript.sml`'s Part 7 comment for the full account, including a
more likely reason no tested goal exercises this disjunct: how
`FLOOKUP`/`is_fmap'` are actually dispatched at a typedef instance,
`Refute_ModelFinder_HOL.sml`'s `synthetic_fmap_typedef`).
`FUPDATE`/`FEMPTY`/`FAPPLY`/`FDOM` each get their own ersatz row
(`fupdate'`/`fempty'`/`fapply'`/`fdom'`) built on `FLOOKUP` and
`combin$UPDATE`/`combin$K`; `FAPPLY` off its finite map's domain reads
`unknown` rather than an arbitrary value.  At a non-`bool` range this
lands `unknown` at a value position, so the problem-wide `unknown_value`
totality veto (`Refute_ModelFinder_Kodkod.sml`) fires and costs
`NoCounterexample` outright for *any* goal mentioning `FAPPLY` at that
instance, whether or not its truth actually depends on the off-domain
read.  At `bool` range `is_unknown`'s argument stays `Formula`-typed and
the veto never sees a value-position occurrence to fire on; a genuinely
true off-domain fact there is instead decided through the ordinary
sound/liberal problem split (`Refute_ModelFinder.sml`), which leaves a
falsely-total goal `Potential`/unconfirmed rather than certifying it,
not through this veto.  `abs_fmap'` is Hilbert choice, so it
is a genuine inverse of `FLOOKUP` only where a preimage exists;
`synthetic_fmap_typedef` (`Refute_ModelFinder_HOL.sml`) supplies the two
theorems saying exactly that as the typedef's inverse axioms, the
structurally correct thing for a typedef registration to carry -- the
same role a validated typedef's own bijection theorem fills -- even
though at a finite key type the encoder's own identification of the
abstract carrier with the represented subset of `'a -> 'b option`
already makes `FLOOKUP` injective there without them; see
`refuteScript.sml`'s Part 7 comment.  The witness-generated
`FEMPTY |+ ...` display
(`fmap_atom_to_chain`, `Refute_ModelFinder_Model.sml`) only fires when
the underlying chain's base is literal `NONE` or the reconstruction's
irrelevant-value marker; a chain bottoming out at the unknown-value
marker, or containing a duplicate key, is shown as the raw `abs_fmap'`
rep term instead of guessed at.

Hilbert choice (`min$@`) is otherwise encoded as an uninterpreted function
pinned only by `refuteTheory`'s `Eps_psimp`, which is vacuous once no
in-scope value satisfies the predicate; reading an atom off that
unconstrained relation would silently turn a sound problem into a
countermodel of a true theorem (`x <> y ==> CARD {x; y} = 2` via `card'`'s
witness list, and a goal naming `relation$the_fun` directly rather than
through `WFREC`: the `wfrec'` row above intercepts only the `WFREC`
symbol, and `the_fun` is a public constant of `relationTheory` in its
own right that still unfolds straight into `@f. approx R M x f`).
`unfold_defs_in_term`
(`Refute_ModelFinder_HOL.sml`) inserts a guard at every syntactic
occurrence -- goal and harvested axiom alike -- with a one-shot rewrite:
`$@ P` becomes `if (?x. P x) then $@ P else unknown`, so an empty
in-scope extension reports the value as unrepresented rather than
arbitrary.  This is a `[deviation from upstream]`: Isabelle's `Eps_psimp`
has the identical hole.  `refute$safe_The` (the definite-description
counterpart also recognized by `is_descr`) is deliberately excluded from
the guard: as a genuine description its empty extension already yields an
empty relation, so it is sound as encoded.

"Every syntactic occurrence is guarded" describes insertion, not the final
encoded problem: a later pass, `destroy_existential_equalities`
(`Refute_ModelFinder_Preproc.sml`), can collapse a guard's own `?x. x = e`
condition to `T` and erase the `if` it introduced.  This stays sound for
two independent reasons.  The bounds signal below is set at insertion
time, so it survives the erasure regardless of what happens to the `if`.
And the erased case is itself sound on the same negative-polarity grounds
as the exact-domain escape below: `e` is either in scope, in which case
the guard's condition would have been true anyway, or unrepresented, in
which case `P`'s in-scope reads are `unknown` rather than false and
`Eps_psimp` still constrains `$@ P`.

Two escapes stand the guard down where the unguarded read is independently
sound.  Provably-empty: when `~(?x. P x)` is a HOL theorem, HOL itself
leaves `$@ P` unspecified, so any value is a correct reading; decided by a
fixed, terminating decision procedure (`bossLib.DECIDE`), never a
wall-clock-bounded search.  Cost is not a function of size: `DECIDE`
expands a case-splitting arithmetic generator (subtraction, `PRE`, `DIV`,
`MOD`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `EXP`, a numeral-typed `COND`, a non-numeral `*`)
combinatorially, so the attempt is gated by a syntactic fragment check
first -- any of those generators, or a quantifier, anywhere in the
predicate keeps the guard without ever calling `DECIDE` -- then a size
ceiling on what the fragment accepts, then a per-context cap on the
number of distinct predicates `DECIDE` is ever called on in one Refute
call; declining at any gate just keeps the guard.  Memoised per predicate
(`Term.aconv`) since a harvested axiom's predicate can recur across many
specialized occurrences.  Exact domain: when `P`'s domain type is `bool`,
every model this module builds interprets `bool` with exactly its two HOL
elements, never subject to card search or completeness approximation, so
no in-scope extension can miss a HOL witness by omitting a domain
element.  A domain element's own read of `P` can still come back
`unknown` rather than false; the escape is sound there too, but not
because an unknown read is discarded.  Asserted positively, `Eps_psimp`
(`P x ==> ~P y ==> ($@ P = y) ==> ($@ P = x)`) puts `P x` at negative
polarity, and the encoder resolves an unknown or unrepresented read at
negative polarity to `True` rather than ignoring it
(`unknown_formula`/`formula_from_opt_atom`,
`Refute_ModelFinder_Kodkod.sml`), so an unknown read still fires the axiom
and forces `$@ P` onto a genuine witness -- the escape is sound because
unknown reads strengthen the axiom, not because they vacate it.  Per-type
scope exactness for other types is not reachable at this point of the
pipeline (`unfold_defs_in_term` runs during preprocessing, before any
scope is chosen), so `bool` is the one case decided statically.  This also
removes the cost of `bool$COND`'s own defining axiom, which the guard
would otherwise reach every time it is harvested
(`arity_of_built_in_const`, `Refute_ModelFinder_HOL.sml`).

A fired guard means a scope may have truncated a real witness, so a clean
search that finds no model may not conclude whole-space exhaustion from it:
`choice_guard_inserted`, set whenever the guard is inserted anywhere in a
problem, vetoes `NoCounterexample` in `total_scope_search`
(`Refute_ModelFinder.sml`) the same way the scope-completeness check already
does for a type that is never exact (`num`, `int`, words) -- the search
instead reports a bounds-relative `Unknown`, and the adaptive scope search
keeps growing rather than concluding.  This is a deliberate, static,
per-problem approximation, not a recomputation per scope.

One corpus shape is a deliberate divergence from upstream rather than a bug
to match: `($@ (\j. j > SUC 2 /\ j <= 4)) = x ==> x = 4` and its `x <> 0`
sibling are HOL theorems -- the predicate is a num singleton, so
`SELECT_AX` forces `$@ P = 4` regardless of which value choice picks -- but
Isabelle reports `Genuine` at a scope too small to contain 4, the same
scope-arbitrary-read defect this guard exists to close.  HolRefute reports
`Unknown` instead and does not reproduce the imported bug.

Recorded follow-ups are compute-hosted residual-inspection narrowing; full
function flattening; and higher-order and negative smart-generator modes.
These are not part of the current implementation.
